.SH NAME
md_ARCHITECTURE \- 🏛️ SystemEx Architecture Overview 
.PP


.PP
\fBSystemEx\fP is a modular, deterministic low‑level framework for \&.NET\&. 
.br
 Its architecture is intentionally divided into isolated subsystems to ensure stability, clarity, and predictable behavior\&.
.SH "🧱 Core Principles"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Deterministic behavior 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Zero hidden allocations 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Explicit numeric semantics 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
STL‑style algorithms and iterators 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Modular subsystems with minimal cross‑dependencies 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Engine‑oriented design (numeric, threading, random, interop) 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Debug‑friendly structure 
.br

.PP
.SH "🧩 Module Overview"
.PP
.SS "SystemEx (Base Layer)"
Foundational primitives: \fRBuffer\fP, \fRFlexSpan\fP, \fRMath\fP, ranges, base tuples\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Collections\&.Generic"
High‑performance containers, node‑based traversal\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Collections\&.Model"
Higher‑level abstractions built on generic collections\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Algorithms"
STL‑style algorithms operating on \fBSystemEx\fP iterators\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Drawing"
HDR color models, HSV, RGB, CMY, XYZ, YUV, NCol, light models, canvas utilities\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Hash"
Hashing utilities: Bernstein, HashFactory, HashAttribute\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.IO"
Deterministic IO streams: \fRCacheStream\fP, \fRWriteStream\fP\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Numeric"
Custom numeric types (Half16, Half16b, Ratio, Fast_Int, Fast_Byte), vectors, matrices, AxisAngle, projections\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Random"
ISAAC engine, Randx, SeedMixer, HashedSeed\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Runtime"
Cross‑platform dynamic loading and interop utilities\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Threading"
Lightweight threading primitives: EventGroup, LightTask, spinlocks\&.
.SS "SystemEx\&.Utils / Utils\&.Bits"
General utilities, bit operations, layout validation, random helpers\&.
.SH "🧱 Design Choice: Controlled Duplication"
.PP
Some structures exist in multiple forms (e\&.g\&., \fRMap<TKey, TValue>\fP using \fRPair<TKey, TValue>[]\fP instead of \fRVector<Pair<\&.\&.\&.>>\fP)\&. 
.br
 This is intentional:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Prevents deep dependency chains 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Keeps subsystems isolated 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Makes debugging easier 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Allows safe experimentation without affecting other modules 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Reduces risk of regressions in a large generic codebase 
.br

.PP
.SH "Build Flags and Development Channels"
.PP
\fBSystemEx\fP uses build‑flags to separate stable functionality from experimental work\&. The primary flag is:
.SS "\fRUSE_DEVBUILD_UNSTABLE\fP"
This flag enables experimental subsystems, unfinished modules, and active development code\&. These blocks are wrapped in:

.PP
.PP
.nf
#if USE_DEVBUILD_UNSTABLE
    // experimental code
#endif
.fi
.PP

.PP
The purpose of this flag is architectural isolation:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Clean repository: unstable code does not pollute stable modules
.IP "\(bu" 2
Safe iteration: new ideas can be tested without affecting existing APIs
.IP "\(bu" 2
Debuggability: experimental code paths remain invisible in normal builds
.IP "\(bu" 2
Controlled exposure: contributors must opt‑in to unstable features
.IP "\(bu" 2
Reduced regression risk: stable modules remain unaffected by ongoing work
.PP

.PP
Build‑flag isolation ensures that new subsystems can be developed safely without destabilizing the stable architecture\&.

.PP
Development Channels \fBSystemEx\fP conceptually distinguishes two build channels:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Stable Build (default) 
.br
 Contains only production‑ready modules\&. No experimental code is compiled\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Unstable Devbuild (USE_DEVBUILD_UNSTABLE) 
.br
 Contains experimental features, prototypes, debugging utilities, and unfinished subsystems\&. Intended for contributors and advanced users\&.
.PP

.PP
This separation keeps the architecture predictable, maintainable, and safe for long‑term evolution\&.
.SH "🧩 Maintainer Note"
.PP
\fBSystemEx\fP is developed by a single author (pronouns: dey/deren/dem/dem)\&. 
.br
 The architecture reflects the need for clarity, stability, and maintainability in a solo‑maintained project\&.

.PP
Contributions and technical discussions are welcome\&. 
